<article class="prose article">
    <h1>About pinch.to, the shortest URL shortener</h1>

    <p>pinch.to turns any web address into a link of seven characters, like <code>🤏.to/ꙮ𐐀</code>. It is free, needs
        no account and shows no ads.</p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p><strong>The domain.</strong> <code>🤏.to</code> is a real domain: the pinching-hand emoji on Tonga's
        <code>.to</code> ending. Domain names can only travel as plain ASCII, so behind the scenes browsers turn it into
        <code>xn--rp9h.to</code>, a format called punycode, and back into 🤏 for display.</p>
    <p><strong>The code.</strong> Most shorteners make codes from the 62 Latin letters and digits. pinch.to picks two
        letters at random from about 131,000 letters in Unicode's scripts: Greek, Cyrillic, Hangul, Ethiopic, Cherokee,
        Old Italic and many more. That gives about 17 billion possible codes. Codes never use punctuation, symbols or
        invisible characters, which could break a link or make two links look alike.</p>
    <p><strong>The redirect.</strong> Opening a pinch.to link counts the click and sends you on to the original
        address. Every code also works on <code>pinch.to</code>, so <code>pinch.to/ꙮ𐐀</code> and
        <code>🤏.to/ꙮ𐐀</code> are the same link, for apps that do not recognise an emoji domain as a link.</p>

    <h2>What it is good for, and what not</h2>
    <p>The links are short to look at: in a post, on a slide, on a sticker or a business card. They are not easy to type. Browsers send <code>🤏.to/ꙮ𐐀</code> as
        <code>xn--rp9h.to/%EA%99%AE%F0%90%90%80</code>, and few keyboards have a <code>ꙮ</code>.
        <a href="/blog/shortest-url-shorteners">See how pinch.to compares with other shorteners.</a></p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Every link shows up in the public list of recent links on the home page, with its click count. Do not
            shorten anything private.</li>
        <li>No trackers, analytics or ads. The pages load nothing from other sites: QR codes are drawn by pinch.to, and
            the small site icons in the list are fetched once per domain and served from here.</li>
        <li>pinch.to stores how often each link was opened, not who opened it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>pinch.to is written in Rust, and its source code is on GitHub at
        <a href="https://github.com/vongaisberg/unishort">vongaisberg/unishort</a>. Questions, bug reports and ideas are
        welcome there as issues.</p>
</article>
